s) and exit lambdas (when points leave? Actually point_exit_lambdas are given per cluster, not per point. The cluster has a list of point_exit_lambdas, one for each point? The size is the number of points, and point_exit_lambdas length might be size? Let's check: c0 size=7, point_exit_lambdas length=3; c1 size=4, length=3; c2 size=2, length=1. So the lengths are not equal to size. So point_exit_lambdas are not per point; maybe they are the lambdas at which points exit? The cluster has a birth_lambda, and points exit at various lambdas. Possibly the stability is the sum over points of (1/exit_lambda - 1/birth_lambda) if exit_lambda > birth_lambda? But we don't have per-point exit lambdas; we have a list of exit lambdas for the cluster, maybe representing the points that exit? The size is the number of points in the cluster at birth? Actually cluster size is given, but the point_exit_lambdas list has fewer entries. Perhaps the cluster loses points over time, and the points that remain until the end? Not clear.\n\nAnother interpretation: The cluster is a node in a dendrogram. birth_lambda is the lambda at which the cluster is formed (by merging two subclusters or from points). point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points leave the cluster (i.e., the cluster splits or points are assigned to other clusters?). But the parent mapping gives the tree structure. Possibly each cluster's stability is computed as the sum over its points of (1/persistence) where persistence is the difference between the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster and the lambda at which it entered? But we don't have per-point data.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to compute stability as the average of (death_lambda - birth_lambda) for each point? Death lambda is the point's exit from the cluster. But the exit lambdas list might be for all points that eventually leave? The cluster size is the number of points at birth, and some points may stay until infinity? For cluster c0, birth=0.2, size=7, exit lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9]. That could mean three points leave at those lambdas, and the remaining 4 points stay? But then cluster c1 has birth=0.5, size=4, exit [0.7,1.0,1.2] - three points leave, one stays? c2 birth=0.8, size=2, exit [1.1] - one leaves, one stays.\n\nThen stability might be the sum over points of (1/lambda_exit - 1/lambda_birth) but for points that never exit, maybe we use infinity? Not defined.\n\nMaybe the stability formula is given in the task description? But we only have the public fixtures. The task is DATA-HDB-02, likely from a benchmark. Possibly we need to compute the \"excess of mass\" or \"stability\" as defined in HDBSCAN? HDBSCAN uses the concept of \"cluster stability\" which is sum over points of (1/lambda_max - 1/lambda_min) for the cluster's lifetime. But that requires a dendrogram. For a given cluster, its stability is the sum of (1/lambda_parent - 1/lambda_cluster) for each point? Actually HDBSCAN's stability is defined as: for each cluster, stability = sum_{p in cluster} (1/lambda_{p,parent} - 1/lambda_{p,cluster}) where lambda_{p,cluster} is the lambda at which the point first belongs to that cluster (i.e., the cluster's birth lambda) and lambda_{p,parent} is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (i.e., the lambda at which it becomes part of a larger cluster or gets assigned to a different cluster). In a tree representation, each point has a lambda at which it is first assigned to a cluster (the cluster's birth) and a lambda at which it leaves (the parent cluster's birth or its death). For the root cluster, the point never leaves so lambda_parent is infinite.\n\nIn our input, we have clusters with birth_lambda and list of point_exit_lambdas. Possibly each point_exit_lambda corresponds to a point that leaves at that lambda, and the cluster's size is the number of points it has at birth. Then for points that never leave, we assume they leave at infinity (1/infinity = 0). Then stability for cluster c would be sum over its points of (1/lambda_exit - 1/lambda_birth). For points that leave at a given lambda, contribution is (1/exit_lambda - 1/birth_lambda). For points that never leave (size - number of exit_lambdas), contribution is (0 - 1/birth_lambda) = -1/birth_lambda. That would often be negative, which is unusual because stability is usually positive. So maybe it's (1/birth_lambda - 1/exit_lambda) for points that leave, and for points that stay, (1/birth_lambda - 0) = 1/birth_lambda, positive. That would be sum over points of (1/birth_lambda - 1/exit_lambda) where exit_lambda is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster; if it never leaves, 1/exit_lambda=0. That seems plausible: points that stay longer contribute more stability. In HDBSCAN, stability is sum over points of (1/lambda_parent - 1/lambda_cluster). lambda_parent is the lambda at which the point stops belonging to that cluster (i.e., the point is assigned to a parent cluster). For the root, lambda_parent is infinity. So if we consider each point's exit_lambda as the point's \"parent\" lambda, then stability = sum (1/exit_lambda - 1/birth_lambda). But careful: In HDBSCAN, if a point leaves the cluster at lambda_parent, then it is no longer in the cluster after that, so the point's contribution to stability is (1/lambda_parent - 1/lambda_cluster). Since lambda_parent > lambda_cluster, the contribution is positive. And for points that never leave (i.e., remain until infinity), lambda_parent = infinity, so contribution = 0 - 1/lambda_cluster = -1/lambda_cluster, which would be negative. Actually, in HDBSCAN, points that are in the root cluster (the entire set) have no parent, and their stability is not counted. The stability is only for non-root clusters. For a leaf cluster, points leave at the lambda where they merge into the parent, so that's positive. For a cluster that is not a leaf, points may leave at different lambdas (when they split off). In the tree, each point's exit lambda from a cluster is the birth lambda of the next cluster it belongs to. So for cluster c, its points have exit lambdas that are either the birth lambdas of its child clusters or infinity if it's the root. So stability = sum (1/exit_lambda - 1/birth_lambda). For points that go to child clusters, exit_lambda is child's birth_lambda. For points that never leave? In a hierarchy, if a cluster has children, points are partitioned among children? Actually, each point belongs to exactly one leaf cluster eventually. At the root, all points are present. As you go down, points are assigned to subclusters. So a point will leave a cluster when it becomes part of a subcluster. So for a cluster, the points that remain until the cluster ends (i.e., the cluster's own leaf points) will have exit_lambda equal to the cluster's death? In HDBSCAN, clusters are extracted based on stability, and the stability of a cluster is the sum over all points in the cluster of (1/lambda_birth_of_parent - 1/lambda_birth_of_cluster). But the parent of a cluster is the cluster that contains it at a higher lambda.\n\nGiven the input structure: clusters have a birth_lambda, and point_exit_lambdas list. The parent mapping defines the tree: c0 is root (parent null), c1 and c2 are children of c0. So the dendrogram: c0 (birth=0.2) splits into c1 (birth=0.5) and c2 (birth=0.8) and possibly some points that stay in c0? But c0 size=7, c1 size=4, c2 size=2, total children size=6, so one point remains in c0? But c0's point_exit_lambdas list has three values: 0.4,0.6,0.9. Those might be the lambdas at which points leave c0 to form subclusters? Actually, c1's birth=0.5, c2's birth=0.8. The exit lambdas of c0 are [0.4,0.6,0.9]. That doesn't exactly match. Maybe the points that exit from c0 are the ones that become part of c1 and c2? But c1's birth is 0.5, so points that leave c0 at 0.4 (before c1 is born) doesn't make sense. Possibly the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points leave the cluster to form a new cluster (i.e., the birth of the child cluster). But then we have exit_lambda=0.4, but no child has birth=0.4. So maybe not.\n\nAnother possibility: The dendrogram is not binary; points can leave individually. The point_exit_lambdas might be the lambdas at which individual points are merged into the cluster from below? Or the lambdas at which points are added to the cluster? The birth_lambda is when the cluster appears. Points may join the cluster at later lambdas? In hierarchical clustering, points join a cluster at its birth, and can leave at the birth of a parent cluster? That is confusing.\n\nGiven the limited information, we need to deduce the intended algorithm. Perhaps this is a known problem: DATA-HDB-02 from the Effectslice benchmark. The task is to select clusters based on stability and size threshold. The output includes stability for each cluster and selected cluster_ids. The public fixtures show three identical payloads except nonce. That suggests a deterministic algorithm. The min_cluster_size=3. So clusters with size <3 are not selected? c2 size=2 <3, so it should be excluded. The selected clusters might be those with high stability that are not nested? Typically in HDBSCAN, the cluster selection uses stability to decide which clusters to keep (e.g., by comparing parent and children stability). So we need to compute stability for each cluster, then select the best set: for each cluster, if its stability is greater than the sum of stabilities of its children, select it and prune children; otherwise keep children.\n\nGiven the input, we can compute stability for each cluster using the formula: stability = sum_{p in cluster} (1/lambda_exit - 1/lambda_birth). But we need exit lambdas for each point. The point_exit_lambdas list gives exit lambdas for some points. The number of points that have exit lambdas is the length of that list. The remaining (size - len(exit_lambdas)) points presumably stay in the cluster until the end (i.e., until infinity). So for those, lambda_exit = infinity, so 1/lambda_exit = 0. Then stability = sum(1/exit for each exit lambda) + (size - len(exit_lambdas))*0 - (size)*(1/birth) ??? Wait, careful: For each point, contribution is (1/exit_lambda - 1/birth_lambda). If point has exit lambda, use that; else exit lambda = infinity => 1/exit = 0. So total stability = sum_{exit in exit_lambdas} (1/exit - 1/birth) + (size - len(exit_lambdas)) * (0 - 1/birth) = (1/birth)*[size - len(exit_lambdas)]? Actually factoring: sum over all points of -1/birth is -size/birth. Then add sum of 1/exit for those that have exit. So stability = (sum_{exit in point_exit_lambdas} 1/exit) - size/birth_lambda.\n\nCheck for c0: size=7, birth=0.2, exits [0.4,0.6,0.9]. Sum 1/exit = 2.5 + 1.6667 + 1.1111 = 5.2778 approx. size/birth = 7/0.2 = 35. So stability = 5.2778 - 35 = -29.7222 negative. That seems unlikely for stability.\n\nMaybe it's the other way: (1/birth - 1/exit) for exits, and for points that stay, (1/birth - 0) = 1/birth. So stability = (size)*(1/birth) - sum_{exit}(1/exit). For c0: 35 - 5.2778 = 29.7222 positive. That makes more sense as a positive measure. So stability = size/birth_lambda - sum(1/exit_lambda). For points that never exit, they contribute 1/birth each (since 1/infinity=0). For those that exit, they contribute (1/birth - 1/exit). So total stability = (size/birth) - sum(1/exit). That is a plausible stability measure: the total \"excess of mass\" over the death points. In HDBSCAN, the stability of a cluster is usually defined as sum over points of (1/lambda_death - 1/lambda_birth) where lambda_death is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (i.e., the point's death in that cluster). For points that stay until the end, lambda_death = infinity, so 1/lambda_death = 0, contribution = -1/lambda_birth? That gives negative. So HDBSCAN actually uses the opposite: stability = sum over points of (1/lambda_birth - 1/lambda_death). That is positive, as we computed. Yes, that matches: stability = sum_{points} (1/lambda_birth - 1/lambda_death). For points that never die, lambda_death = infinity, contribution = 1/lambda_birth. So our formula stability = (size/birth) - sum(1/exit) is correct if exit corresponds to death.\n\nNow, we need to apply it to each cluster. For cluster c0: size=7, birth=0.2, exits=[0.4,0.6,0.9]. sum(1/exit)=1/0.4+1/0.6+1/0.9 = 2.5+1.6666667+1.1111111 = 5.2777778. So stability = 7/0.2 - 5.2777778 = 35 - 5.2777778 = 29.7222222.\nFor c1: size=4, birth=0.5, exits=[0.7,1.0,1.2]. sum(1/exit)=1/0.7+1/1.0+1/1.2 ≈ 1.4285714+1+0.8333333=3.2619047. stability = 4/0.5 - 3.2619047 = 8 - 3.2619047 = 4.7380953.\nFor c2: size=2, birth=0.8, exits=[1.1]. sum(1/exit)=0.9090909. stability = 2/0.8 - 0.9090909 = 2.5 - 0.9090909 = 1.5909091.\n\nNow, we have a tree with parent mapping. The root is c0 (parent null). Children: c1 and c2. The min_cluster_size=3, so c2 (size=2) should be ignored? In HDBSCAN, clusters below min_cluster_size are not considered for selection. They might be included in the output list but not selected? The output schema requires all clusters in the clusters array? It says \"clusters\" is an array of objects with cluster_id, size, stability. It doesn't say filtered by min_cluster_size. The selected_cluster_ids is a separate list. So likely all clusters from the input should appear in the output list (including those below min_cluster_size) because they are part of the hierarchy. Then selected_cluster_ids are those that are selected based on stability and size.\n\nThe selection algorithm: We need to select a set of clusters that covers the points and maximizes total stability, subject to min_cluster_size constraint (typically clusters below min_cluster_size are not selected). The classic HDBSCAN cluster selection: For each node, compute whether to select it or its children by comparing stability. If node stability > sum of stabilities of its descendants? Actually, we compare node's stability to the sum of stabilities of its selected children. The algorithm: Starting from leaves, for each node, if sum of selected children stabilities > node stability, select children (propagate up) else select node. But we need to handle root specially.\n\nGiven the tree: c0 root, c1 and c2 children. c2 size=2 <3, so c2 cannot be selected even if its stability is high? Usually min_cluster_size is a hard constraint: only clusters with size >= min_cluster_size can be selected. So c2 is ineligible. c1 size=4 >=3, eligible. So we compare c0 stability (29.72) vs sum of eligible children stabilities? But c2 is ineligible, so only c1's stability (4.74) is considered. Since c0 stability > c1 stability, we select c0, and discard c1 and c2? But then we lose c1 and c2 points. However, selection should cover all points? In HDBSCAN, the selected clusters are a partition of the data points. So we need to decide if c0 is selected, then all points in c0 are covered, and c1 and c2 are not selected. That seems plausible. Alternatively, if c1 had higher stability than c0, we would select c1 and also c2 if it were eligible? But c2 is child of c0 as well? Actually both c1 and c2 are children of c0, not nested. So they are siblings. The decision is between selecting the parent or the children. Since c0's stability is greater than sum of its children's stabilities (only c1 eligible, sum = 4.74), we select c0. So selected_cluster_ids = [\"c0\"].\n\nBut there is also the possibility that a child cluster might have its own children? Not in this simple tree. So likely answer: clusters array includes all three clusters with computed stability, selected_cluster_ids = [\"c0\"].\n\nBut we must ensure the stability formula is correct. The public fixtures show multiple identical cases, so the solution is deterministic.\n\nWe need to write solve(case) that takes the payload and returns the required JSON. The output must have clusters (list of objects) and selected_cluster_ids.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n1. Input payload has: clusters list, parent map, min_cluster_size.\n2. For each cluster in payload['clusters'], compute stability = size/birth_lambda - sum(1/exit for each in point_exit_lambdas). Note: birth_lambda may be zero? Not likely. Use float division.\n3. Build a tree structure: keyed by cluster_id, with parent pointer, list of children.\n4. Perform a post-order traversal to compute for each node the total stability of its subtree for selection decisions. But we need to decide selection: For each node, compute whether to select it based on comparing its stability to sum of stabilities of its selected children (only children that are eligible and have passed the selection process). However, we need to determine eligible: clusters with size < min_cluster_size cannot be selected. They are still present in the tree but cannot be selected as final clusters. So for a node with children, we consider only children that are either selected or could be selected? The typical HDBSCAN algorithm: For each node, compute the \"excess of mass\" and then propagate. Simpler: We can implement the selection recursively: For a node, if its size < min_cluster_size, it cannot be selected; we must select its descendants (if any) that can cover its points. But if the node itself is ineligible, its children might be eligible. The selection should produce a set of clusters that partition the points and are all >= min_cluster_size. The algorithm: Starting from leaves, for each node, compute the candidate selected set from its subtree. For a node, if its size < min_cluster_size, then it cannot be a candidate, so the selected set is the union of the selected sets of its children (if they cover all points). If its size >= min_cluster_size, then we have two options: either select the node itself, or keep the union of its children's selected sets. Choose the option with higher stability: stability(node) vs sum of stabilities of children's selected sets. But we need the actual selected cluster IDs, not just stability sum. So we need to propagate the set.\n\nGiven the tree is small (only three clusters), we can implement a recursive function that returns a tuple: (selected_cluster_ids list, total_stability) for that subtree, assuming we only consider clusters with size >= min_cluster_size. For each node:\n- Compute children results recursively.\n- If node's size < min_cluster_size, then it cannot be selected, so the result is the union of children's results (since points must be covered by children if possible). However, if a child subtree has no selected cluster (because its size also < min_cluster_size?), then that subtree would have no selected clusters, which is problematic because points would be uncovered. But min_cluster_size ensures that eventually we reach clusters that are large enough? Not necessarily. But in HDBSCAN, leaves are points themselves, but here clusters are given with sizes. For simplicity, we assume that for nodes with size < min_cluster_size, we must still select something from their descendants. But if the node has no children, and its size < min_cluster_size, it cannot be selected, and there is no way to cover its points because points are not further subdivided. In that case, the cluster would be considered noise or merged? Possibly such clusters are simply not selected and points are considered noise. But the problem might expect that all clusters are represented in the output (clusters list), but selected_cluster_ids only includes those that meet criteria. Points from non-selected clusters might be assigned to parent? In this fixture, c2 size=2 <3, so it cannot be selected. Its parent c0 is selected, so its points are covered by c0. So that's fine. For a leaf cluster below min_cluster_size, if its parent is also below min_cluster_size, then it might be a problem. But we can assume that the input is such that selection is possible.\n\nGiven the fixture, we can just implement the algorithm:\n- Build mapping from cluster_id to cluster data.\n- Build children mapping: parent -> list of children.\n- Find root (parent null).\n- Define recursive function process(node_id):\n   - Get node data and compute its stability (as above).\n   - Get children.\n   - For each child, recursively get (selected_from_child, child_total_stab).\n   - If node size < min_cluster_size:\n        - total_stab = sum(child_total_stab for all children)\n        - selected = union of selected_from_child lists\n   - Else:\n        - child_sum_stab = sum(child_total_stab)\n        - if node_stability >= child_sum_stab:\n            total_stab = node_stability\n            selected = [node_id]\n        - else:\n            total_stab = child_sum_stab\n            selected = union\n- Return selected list.\n\nWe must ensure stability is computed as float. Use Python's fractions? But float is fine.\n\nNow output format: clusters list must include all clusters from input (with computed stability), not filtered. selected_cluster_ids is list of strings.\n\nWe need to produce a JSON response per the schema: { \"clusters\": [...], \"selected_cluster_ids\": [...] }.\n\nNow implement solve(case) in Python. The case is the payload object.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters_input = case['clusters']\n    parent = case['parent']\n    # Build lookup\n    cluster_by_id = {}\n    for cl in clusters_input:\n        cl_id = cl['cluster_id']\n        # compute stability\n        size = cl['size']\n        birth = cl['birth_lambda']\n        exits = cl['point_exit_lambdas']\n        # stability = size/birth - sum(1/exit)\n        stab = size / birth - sum(1.0 / ex for ex in exits)\n        cluster_by_id[cl_id] = {\n            'size': size,\n            'birth': birth,\n            'exits': exits,\n            'stability': stab,\n            'children': []\n        }\n    # Build tree\n    root = None\n    for cl_id, data in cluster_by_id.items():\n        p = parent.get(cl_id)  # may be None\n        if p is None:\n            root = cl_id\n        else:\n            cluster_by_id[p]['children'].append(cl_id)\n    # Recursive selection\n    def process(node_id):\n        node = cluster_by_id[node_id]\n        children = node['children']\n        child_selected = []\n        child_stab_sum = 0.0\n        for child_id in children:\n            sel, stab = process(child_id)\n            child_selected.extend(sel)\n            child_stab_sum += stab\n        if node['size'] < min_size:\n            # cannot select this node\n            return child_selected, child_stab_sum\n        else:\n            node_stab = node['stability']\n            if node_stab >= child_stab_sum:\n                return [node_id], node_stab\n            else:\n                return child_selected, child_stab_sum\n    selected_ids, _ = process(root)\n    # Build output clusters list: for each input cluster, output cluster_id, size, stability\n    output_clusters = []\n    for cl in clusters_input:\n        cl_id = cl['cluster_id']\n        data = cluster_by_id[cl_id]\n        output_clusters.append({\n            'cluster_id': cl_id,\n            'size': data['size'],\n            'stability': round(data['stability'], 6)  # maybe not needed, but to avoid floating point issues\n        })\n    return {\n        'clusters': output_clusters,\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_ids\n    }\n\nBut careful: The recursion assumes the tree is a tree (acyclic) and root is defined. Also parent mapping may only have entries for clusters that have parent; root has null. That's fine.\n\nEdge cases: If there are multiple roots? Not expected.\n\nWe need to ensure that the stability computation matches the expected. The public fixtures show repeated cases, so the answer should be consistent.\n\nWe can test with the given payload:\n- c0: size=7, birth=0.2, exits=[0.4,0.6,0.9], stability=29.722222... \n- c1: size=4, birth=0.5, exits=[0.7,1.0,1.2], stability=4.738095...\n- c2: size=2, birth=0.8, exits=[1.1], stability=1.590909...\nmin_size=3.\nProcess root c0:\n  children: c1, c2.\n  Process c1: leaf, no children, size=4>=3, node_stab=4.738, child_sum=0, so select c1 -> [c1], stab=4.738.\n  Process c2: leaf, size=2<3, cannot select, child_sum=0, so return [], 0.0.\n  child_selected = ['c1'], child_stab_sum=4.738.\n  node_stab(c0)=29.722 > child_stab_sum, so select c0 => selected_ids=['c0'], totalstab=29.722.\nSo selected cluster ids = [\"c0\"].\n\nThat seems plausible.\n\nNow we must ensure the output JSON matches schema exactly. The clusters array must contain objects with cluster_id, size, stability. We will include all clusters from input.\n\nNow write the final solution as a JSON string with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code.\n\nWe must escape newlines and quotes for JSON. Use triple quotes? Actually we need to produce a JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case): ...\"}. The Python source must be a string with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll write the code as a multiline string and then escape it. Easier: write the code with proper indentation, then replace newline with \\\\n, backslash with \\\\\\\\, and double quotes with \\\\\" etc. We can do that manually or use a JSON serializer in mind. Since we are the AI, we can generate the escaped string directly.\n\nLet's write the Python code as a single line with escaped newlines? The instruction says \"encoding newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON\". So we can write the code with actual newlines inside the string, but when we construct the JSON, we need to escape them. Easier: write the Python code in a raw string and then manually escape. I'll write it step by step.\n\nThe Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters_input = case['clusters']\n    parent = case['parent']\n    cluster_by_id = {}\n    for cl in clusters_input:\n        cl_id = cl['cluster_id']\n        size = cl['size']\n        birth = cl['birth_lambda']\n        exits = cl['point_exit_lambdas']\n        stab = size / birth - sum(1.0 / ex for ex in exits)\n        cluster_by_id[cl_id] = {'size': size, 'birth': birth, 'exits': exits, 'stability': stab, 'children': []}\n    for cl_id in cluster_by_id:\n        p = parent.get(cl_id)\n        if p is not None:\n            cluster_by_id[p]['children'].append(cl_id)\n    # find root\n    root = None\n    for cl_id in cluster_by_id:\n        if parent.get(cl_id) is None:\n            root = cl_id\n            break\n    def process(node_id):\n        node = cluster_by_id[node_id]\n        child_selected = []\n        child_stab_sum = 0.0\n        for child_id in node['children']:\n            sel, stab = process(child_id)\n            child_selected.extend(sel)\n            child_stab_sum += stab\n        if node['size'] < min_size:\n            return child_selected, child_stab_sum\n        else:\n            node_stab = node['stability']\n            if node_stab >= child_stab_sum:\n                return [node_id], node_stab\n            else:\n                return child_selected, child_stab_sum\n    selected_ids, _ = process(root)\n    output_clusters = []\n    for cl in clusters_input:\n        cl_id = cl['cluster_id']\n        d = cluster_by_id[cl_id]\n        output_clusters.append({'cluster_id': cl_id, 'size': d['size'], 'stability': d['stability']})\n    return {'clusters': output_clusters, 'selected_cluster_ids': selected_ids}\n\nNow we need to escape this for JSON. I'll write it as a multiline string and then use json.dumps on the whole object later? But we are the AI generating text; we can manually create the JSON with escaped newlines.\n\nBetter: I'll write the entire JSON object as a single line with escaped newlines. But to avoid errors, I'll generate a Python dictionary and then serialize it in my mind. Since the response must be exactly one JSON object, I'll write it.\n\nLet's produce the escaped string:\n\nThe code has newlines, so I'll replace each newline with \\\\n. Also backslashes: none in code except division? Actual